data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT","content":"\"The workflow needs to be in JSON format similar to:\\n{\\n    \\\"label\\\": \\\"workflowName\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"workflowDescription\\\",\\n    \\\"inputs\\\": [],\\n    \\\"triggers\\\": [\\n        {\\n            \\\"label\\\": \\\"Manual\\\",\\n            \\\"name\\\": \\\"trigger_1\\\",\\n            \\\"type\\\": \\\"manual/v1/manual\\\"\\n        }\\n    ],\\n    \\\"tasks\\\": []\\n}\\n\\n\\n- A workflow can have one or more triggers, plus as many actions or task dispatchers as needed\\n\\nOutput properties:\\nSome tasks have output properties that you get with getTaskOutputProperty() tool. Output property is a response to the task.\\nFor example: the action getMail will probably have the mail parameters as an output.\\nYou can reference the output property in a format similar to:\\n${taskName.property}\\n${taskName.arrayProperty[1]}\\n${taskName.objectProperty.property}\\n\\nTask parameters:\\n- Fill in every required task parameter whose value is already known from the request instead of leaving it empty.\\n- In particular, when the request names a specific data table, set the dataTable component's \\\"table\\\" parameter to that table's base name (e.g. \\\"table\\\": \\\"invoices\\\").\\n- Only leave a parameter unset when its value genuinely cannot be derived from the request (e.g. it must be picked from the user's account, like a folder or channel id).\\n\"","messageId":"b8302267-da01-4c2c-9736-cbdfa9b04cd3","rawEvent":null,"role":"tool","timestamp":1792280264732,"toolCallId":"b4adbd7e-0a7b-44e6-8d87-22249573e740","type":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT"}

